This is the first design where I've had to move away from IS 2062 E250, which is basically muscle memory for me at this point — it's what I've used on every job so far, and it's sitting in every stockyard back home. So when I saw ASTM A516 Grade 70 called out here, my first honest reaction was "why not just use what I already know and can get easily." Turns out there's a real reason, and it's not about sourcing at all. Writing it down here so that the next time I open A516 or A6, I'm not starting from zero again.

---

## 1. It really comes down to working temperature

IS 2062 is a solid, general-purpose structural grade. It gives you strength and weldability, but it was never written with low-temperature behavior in mind. There's no built-in toughness testing — if you want Charpy data on IS 2062, you're bolting that on as a special request, on a grade that wasn't designed around it in the first place.

A516 says its whole purpose right in the scope: it's meant for **welded pressure vessels where improved notch toughness matters.** That single line explains basically everything else in the spec:

* It's built to pair with **Charpy V-notch testing (Supplementary Requirement S5)** — something you pull in when the design temperature makes brittle fracture a genuine concern.
* The heat treatment rules are tied to the same worry. Anything **over 40 mm thick has to be normalized, full stop, no option to skip it.** Under 40 mm, as-rolled is fine by default — but the moment you invoke Charpy testing, normalizing comes back into the picture unless you specifically say otherwise.

So the chain of logic is: the site's working temperature creates a real risk of brittle fracture at lower service temps -> you need a plate spec that actually has a toughness pathway built in, not bolted on -> that's A516, not IS 2062.

I want to be fair to IS 2062 here though — it's not that you *can't* get impact-tested IS 2062 plate, people do call it out on Indian jobs too. It's more that A516 is built around this from the ground up — the chemistry, the normalizing thresholds, the whole S5 pathway — so it's just a more rigorous and traceable way to handle it when low working temperature is an actual design input, not an afterthought.

