## 4. A6/A6M — the layer sitting underneath A516

This one took a bit to get my head around, mostly because IS 2062 doesn't work this way — it's one document with everything in it. ASTM splits things up: A516 gives you the chemistry and strength numbers, but dimensional tolerances, conditioning/quality rules, heat treatment procedure, terminology — all of that lives in a separate general-requirements spec.

*(Note to self: A516 actually pairs with **A20/A20M** for pressure vessel plate specifically — A6/A6M is the equivalent umbrella spec for general structural bars, plates, shapes, and sheet piling. Worth double-checking which one actually governs before assuming A6 applies here.)*

A few things from A6/A6M worth keeping in mind:

### Definitions (Section 3) that change how you read a drawing
* **Plates vs. Flats:** Split by a thickness/width matrix — over 8 in wide and 0.230 in thick, or over 48 in wide and 0.180 in thick, is a plate; below that it's a flat, which has its own tolerance rules.
* **Edge Types:** Mill edge, universal mill edge, sheared edge, gas cut edge, and special cut edge are all separately defined, and which one you've got decides which tolerance table applies.
* **Steel Types:** Old terms like rimmed / semi-killed / killed / capped steel are defined too — good to recognize if it ever shows up on an older reference drawing.

### Section 9, plate conditioning — numbers to remember
* Grinding to clean up surface defects can't take more than **7% off nominal thickness** (for weight-ordered plate) or drop below **minimum permissible thickness** (for thickness-ordered plate).
* Any chipped, ground, or gouged area is capped at **2% of the total surface area** per piece.
* Weld repair needs a qualified WPS, AWS-spec electrodes, low-hydrogen for SMAW, and inspection both before and after.

### Annex A1, permitted variation tables
Thickness, width, length, weight, camber, flatness, sweep, cross-section — every product type A6 covers gets its own table. Next step is finding the specific thickness/flatness table for whatever plate thickness the tank design actually settles on, since that's what feeds fit-up tolerance for fabrication.

