## Where I've landed

This was never really an IS-vs-ASTM sourcing question. The working temperature on this design pushes brittle fracture into something that actually has to be engineered around, and A516 is built for exactly that — the scope, the Charpy pathway, the mandatory normalizing past 40 mm. IS 2062 is a good grade, but it just doesn't have that architecture. A6/A6M is the rulebook sitting underneath, handling dimensions and quality, the same job A20/A20M does for the plate spec itself. 

And the actual number that decides whether impact testing is required at all doesn't come from A516 at all — it comes from ASME UCS-66, plotting MDMT against governing thickness on the right curve (B for as-rolled, D for normalized). That's the piece that turns "-30°C site" into an actual material decision.
